          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title
          %p
            Bitcoin with only about 8 transaction per second (TPS) and also big latency times (for final confirmations) is not very suitable for most
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blockchain"} blockchain
            applications. Similarly with most early generation distributed ledgers. Important things for performance include also the efficiency of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+machine"} virtual machine
            employed (trend is that the newest projects go toward versions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WebAssembly"} WebAssembly
            VM).
          %p Faster and even scalable solutions are now in works. We first describe improvements on Bitcoin and Ethernet networks in the form of lightning and plasma networks on top of those, and then various projects which claim to be scalable, “3rd generation” etc. Essential improvements are various algorithms trying to go beyond linearity of classical blockchain projects, especially those relying on DAGs (directed acyclic graphs) and on so called sharding. As far as consensus algorithms there are scalability improvements with many new algorithms, while PoW is in a way most secure (for public blockchains).
          %p
            While mining is computationally intensive it is considered that the optimizations with use of GPU and ASIC hardware are bad forces of centralization (not to mention unhealthy and energy consuming chase for success with single purpose hardware). It is considered better to use computational resources for more useful work like validation of smart contracts, security of computation, cryptographic tools for privacy protection and so on. With FPGA the situation is a little better, and some FPGA are claimed to be useful for smart contract processing and alike, see
            %a{:href => "https://www.embedded.com/electronics-blogs/say-what-/4461708/FPGA-based-frameworks-speed-blockchain-processing"} here
            there are also Chaincode
            = succeed "-specific" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+machine"} virtual machine
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://accelor.io"} accelor.io
          %h3#lightning_network Lightning network
          %p Bitcoin solves some of the problems with so called lightning network on top of it, which makes temporary transactions with lots of intermediate transfers only off chain and only small footprint on the main chain, ensuring small price of transactions.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://lightning.network"} lightning.network
            %li
              Joseph Poon, Tadge Dryja,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lightning Network
              %a{:href => "https://lightning.network/\nlightning-network-paper.pdf"} pdf
              Mar 2015.
            %li
              A van Wirdum
              %em
                Understanding the lightning network
                %a{:href => "https://bitcoinmagazine.com/articles/understanding-the-lightning-network-part-building-a-bidirectional-payment-channel-1464710791"} 1
                %a{:href => "https://bitcoinmagazine.com/articles/understanding-the-lightning-network-part-creating-the-network-1465326903"} 2
            %li
              Amazon using lightning network for e-commerce, April 2019
              %a{:href => "https://www.coindesk.com/you-can-now-shop-with-bitcoin-on-amazon-using-lightning"} news
          %h3#ethernet_improvements_and_plasma Ethernet improvements and plasma
          %p
            Ethernet recognizes the same problem as Bitcoin, but has significant plans of substantial upgrades of its Mainnet, namely the new Casper protocol, plans for deployment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WebAssembly"} WebAssembly
            virtual machine and sharding which, according to Buterin, could together bring million TPS when all employed as planned.
          %p In addition, there is an analogue algorithm to lightning network targeted to Ethereum, with substantial new ideas in the algorithm, called plasma network.
          %ul
            %li
              Joseph Poon, Vitalik Buterin,
              %em Plasma: scalable autonomous smart contracts
              %a{:href => "https://www.plasma.io/plasma.pdf"} pdf
              draft whitepaper
          %p There are several realizations of plasma, mostly used being the Loom network.
          %h3#projects_based_on_dags Projects based on DAGs
          %p There are several major algorithms. Several projects are based on “hashgraph” paper
          %ul
            %li
              Leemon Baird,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Swirlds hashgraph consensus algorithm: fair, fast, byzantine fault tolerance
              %a{:href => "https://www.swirlds.com/downloads/SWIRLDS-TR-2016-01.pdf"} pdf
              (May 2016);
              %em Hashgraph consensus: detailed examples
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.swirlds.com/downloads/SWIRLDS-TR-2016-02.pdf"} pdf
              %em Overview of Swirlds Hashgraph: An Introduction To The Hashgraph (SDK Available Now)
              %a{:href => "https://steemit.com/technology/@swirlds/overview-of-swirlds-hashgraph-an-introduction-to-the-hashgraph-sdk-available-now"} online
          %p
            including
            %a{:href => "https://www.hedera.com/whitepaper"} Hedera
            which claims 50000 to 500000 TPS tested,
          %ul
            %li
              Hedera Hashgraph whitepaper
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.hedera.com/hh-whitepaper-v1.5-190219.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.hedera.com/whitepaper"} web
          %p then so called Consensus project etc.
          %p
            Somewhat similar but with a new innovative algorithm is
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.tolar.io"} Tolar Hashnet
            %a{:href => "https://www.tolar.io/static/media/Whitepaper-Tolar.2f13a40e.pdf"} pdf
            with around 150000 TPS achieved on testnet and low power consumption.
          %p
            Another kind of scalable algorithms using DAGs is the tangle blockchain (of the
            %a{:href => "https://www.iota.org"} iota
            project)
          %ul
            %li
              Serguei Popov,
              %em The tangle
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.iotatoken.com/IOTA_Whitepaper.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#telegram_open_network Telegram Open Network
          %p
            %a{:href => "https://telegram.org"} Telegram
            (see
            %a{:href => "https://telegram.org/faq"} FAQ
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(messaging_service"} wikipedia
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Telegram+Open+Network"} Telegram Open Network
            in this wiki and original
          %ul
            %li
              TON whitepaper, 23 page
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://cdn.crowdfundinsider.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/TON-White-Paper-Telegram-ICO.pdf"} pdf
              %a{:href => "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ucUeKg_NiR8RxNAonb8Q55jZha03WC0O/view"} pdf
              %a{:href => "https://icorating.com/upload/whitepaper/gNQ7e9z3lCGi519Wz8mmC0Kg8aA0goeZKAQ802vo.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"}> Nikolai Durov
              ‘s 132-page technical overview
              = succeed "," do
                %em Telegram Open Network
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://toncoin.tech/TON-official-whitepaper.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.kriptovaluta.hr/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/TON-Technology.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %em Telegram Open Network Blockchain
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.docdroid.net/qY4sQEv/telegram-open-network-blockchain-september-5-2018.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %em Telegram Open Network Virtual Machine
              %a{:href => "https://www.docdroid.net/R3vEKBY/telegram-open-network-virtual-machine-september-5-2018.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#redbelly RedBelly
          %p
            Australian academic
            %a{:href => "https://redbellyblockchain.io"} RedBelly
            blockchain (also referred as CSIRO) has confirmed 40000 TPS with Amazon platform worldwide testing. Since mid 2017 it had local tests which could achieve up to 400000 but with all nodes in the same location; see the paper about various performance experiments (the largest performance experiment, with measuring various parameters, with up to 1000 nodes in 10 countries!),
          %ul
            %li
              Concurrent Systems Research Group, University of Sydney, Data61-CSIRO,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Red Belly Blockchain Experiments
              %a{:href => "https://redbellyblockchain.io/papers/redbellyblockchain-experiments.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and selection of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://redbellyblockchain.io/Press.html"} news
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://redbellyblockchain.io/Research.html"} research papers
          %h3#mit_vault MIT Vault
          %ul
            %li
              Derek Leung, Adam Suhl, Yossi Gilad, Nickolai Zeldovich (MIT CSAIL),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vault: fast bootstrapping for cryptocurrencies
              %a{:href => "http://people.csail.mit.edu/nickolai/papers/leung-vault-eprint.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://news.mit.edu/2019/vault-faster-more-efficient-cryptocurrency-0124"} news.mit.edu:faster-more-efficient-cryptocurrency
          %h3#algorand Algorand
          %p
            %a{:href => "https://www.algorand.com"} Algorand
            project, founded by a group at MIT lead by S. Micali, claims 1000 tps and latency of only 5 seconds in a setup with 10000 participants and 500 nodes on testnet world wide. It got 62 M dollars equity funding in 2018 (
            %a{:href => "https://www.the-blockchain.com/2018/10/24/algorand-announces-62m-in-funding-and-appoints-new-executive-team"}> news
            ) and is issuing Algos coins in auction in June 2019,
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://www.the-blockchain.com/2019/06/06/mit-professor-silvio-micalis-algorand-foundation-announces-date-for-first-auction"} news
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/algorand/go-algorand"} github
          %ul
            %li
              Yossi Gilad, Rotem Hemo, Silvio Micali, Georgios Vlachos, Nickolai Zeldovich (MIT CSAIL),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algorand: scaling Byzantine agreements for cryptocurrencies
              %a{:href => "https://people.csail.mit.edu/nickolai/papers/gilad-algorand-eprint.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              Jing Chen, Silvio Micali,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algorand
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.01341"} arxiv/1607.01341
            %li
              github/
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/algobet/kirin"} algobet/kirin
              \- Official javascript framework of the Digital Asset Platform (DAP) protocol atop the POS/POR consensus mechanism. Kirin is the Quarkonium’s first generation constitutional protocols deeply built with the Algorand blockchain
            %li
              S. Gorbounov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algorand releases first open-source code: verifiable random function
              %a{:href => "https://medium.com/algorand/algorand-releases-first-open-source-code-of-verifiable-random-function-93c2960abd61"} medium
            %li
              %em Announcing full open source availability of the Algorand blockchain
